referenceSolutions.java:
class Solution {
  public int sharedDrawersBossReceipt(int[][] drawers) {
    int[][] a=drawers; List<Integer> out=new ArrayList<>();
    {Set<Integer>right=new HashSet<>();for(int x:a[1])right.add(x);Set<Integer>both=new TreeSet<>();for(int x:a[0])if(right.contains(x))both.add(x);out.addAll(both);}
    int answer=0;for(int i=0;i<out.size();i++)answer+=(i+1)*out.get(i);return answer;
  }
}

privateData.editorialMd:
Build shared

`drawers` contains two lists of numbers. Find the numbers that appear in both lists. Put each matching number in `shared` only once. Sort `shared` from smallest to largest.

Return the answer

Multiply each number in `shared` by its position, counting from 1: the first number by 1, the second by 2, and so on. Add those products. Store the sum in `weightedTotal`. Complete `sharedDrawersBossReceipt` so it returns `weightedTotal`. Return it, do not print it.

Why this works

Each value in `shared` follows task 1. The later tasks use only those values. Keeping these steps separate helps avoid returning the list when the question asks for one number. Check empty input, one item, repeated values, and the largest allowed input.

Efficiency goal: time O(n + d log d); extra memory, including the helper list, O(n). n is the combined length of the two lists; d is the number of different values. These labels describe how work and memory grow with the input.

